BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 772.5 Td (In parts of its range, the marsh frog is harvested and traded by humans as a food source, with) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 759.0 Td (its legs being the main part eaten. Some frogs are harvested from the wild, while others are) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 745.5 Td (commercially bred and reared in farms. Frog meat trading is commercially significant on a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 732.0 Td (global scale, with harvested frogs being sold internationally to serve as food. The meat of the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 718.5 Td (marsh frog has high amino acid and nutrient levels, and has thus been determined to be a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 705.0 Td (worthwhile food source for commercial farming. Additionally, the high protein content and low) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 691.5 Td (fat level of frog meat makes it a fitting source of dietary and clinical nutrition. However,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 678.0 Td (frog farms can also harbour pathogens which cause diseases in humans; studies have found that) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 664.5 Td (pathogenic bacteria is common not only on farmed marsh frogs, but also in their water and food,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 651.0 Td (which can pose a risk to the health of both humans and frogs. In particular, one study found) Tj ET
